Z||000000||Formula||04-30-2001||01:41 PM||||I was thinking today, (thinking is bad lol), if, when I graduate, I make $45,000 a year cleared, if I would save my money for 2 years and have around $80,000, if I would buy a house straight out for 50 or 60 thousand, and rent it for $500 a month plus utilities to someone, I would make $6000 a year on it (Im taking interest away to make up for taxes so I'll say $5500 a year). so I open a savings account and save every penny of it.  so in 8 years I would make $44,000, and buy another house to rent at $500 a month.  That would give me $10,100 a year, Save all of that for 4 years and get 40,400, and buy a third and charge $500 a month, and I would be getting 18,000 a year, save that for 3 years and get 54,000 and buy a forth house, this would make my incoming rent 24,000 a year, save that for 2 years and buy a fifth house and make 30,000 a year in rent, save that for 2 years and you would have $60,000, buy a 6th house and get 36000, buy a 7th house and make 42000, 8th and get 48,000, then continue this until you get about 20 houses, (25 years?), and make 120,000 a year and either continue buying houses, retire, or sell the houses for a huge profit (800,000).<P>would this work?<BR>why wouldnt this work?<BR>has any of you done this?<P>   <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigformulagt.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||ferg||04-30-2001||02:15 PM||||I think you're leaving some important stuff out- like your own living expenses. you say you'll clear $45k/year. Ok- so you are going to spend $400/mo for 2 years to live on. ($5,000/yr) That's livin really really cheap. You didn't say if you have student loans to pay back, car payment, fuel costs, toll-road money, maintenance on your car, food, car insurance, health insurance, etc. Think of it- you can only spend $100 per week for all your expenses. Tough to do, in my opinion.<P>Now, lets look at the house- aside from the obvious home cost, you have insurance, mortgage insurance (if bank requires it), maintenance, repairs, etc. Not to mention finding someone to rent the house for $500/mo. You haven't even mentioned making a profit, and if you aren't making money at it -why do it in the first place??????<P>Lets say you can do all of the above, the 2nd and 3rd house you want to buy will cost more due to inflation and homes generally increase in value. <P>I don't know what home prices cost in your area, but in my area- $50-60k for a house will generally buy you a tear down. OR- buy you a house in a bad neighborhood. If that's the case you'll have to put cash into it to make it habitable or if it's a bad area, trying to sell the house could be a problem. <P>It looks good on paper, but I don't think you've thought this all the way through. Since you're in college (guessing early 20's) I don't think you realize how far your money will go. <P>I just graduated a year ago- I'm still trying to find out how to afford my own home, and maintain my current lifestyle. The money doesn't go as far as it should. <P>To answer your original questions<BR>1) I don't think it will work as easily as you state it. (Lots of holes in the game plan)<P>2) See the above issues.<P>3) Yes, people do this all the time- most are older and have disposable income. Not your typical mid 20's individual, with their first real job.<p>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||04-30-2001||02:26 PM||||well saving is the important part of getting rich so your on the right track.but rates are unreal can't be done at that rate of saving to income unless your a hermit.<BR>real estate is good long term plan but why not use a loan to start with instead of total savings before buying??U S  tax laws and loan go to gether to your favor in this. <P>question wonder and stay wierd ray||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||04-30-2001||02:33 PM||||At the same time, <B>"Don't throw the baby out with the bath water"</B> with the way your thinking.<P>Your better off saving and investing - Long Term of course - that same "estimated" money in an agressive market earning between 9 and 22%.  Make alot more and won't loose your shirt after 25 so called years when you try to resell all these so called houses!<P>You also forgot about the prep expense to make it liveable (to your states code), repairs & maintenance expense, insurance, advertising (for when its vacant), property taxes, amenities tax, etc., and other costs when it "does" sit vacant.<P>I don't even see how you could possibly save $80K in two years even if you could retain a base of $45K each year unless you are being paid a hefty 6 digit salary per year.<P>Even if you had to borrow the money, lets say 60K, your monthly payments alone would be between $440.26 and $526.54 per month (figured between 8% and @10% for 30 years respectively).<P>In interest alone you will have paid between $98,493.15 to $129,555.46 respectively over that 30 year time period.<P>Second, when renting a house, your rental value should be between 1 - 2% of the paid value to break even or cover your associated costs.  <P>Therfore, on a $60K house, rent would be between $600 to $1,200 per month.  <P>It better be a darn good house in PA for that price and you better have a lot of well paid renters!<P>IMO, better to have someone else paying you the interest (investing) over time then you paying the bank (mortgage) at the same interest.<P>Invoice is in the mail for this "free" advise.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||04-30-2001||05:30 PM||||Even if you do make 45K per year salary.  You will only bring home 35-40k.  So your plan will work if you spend exactly $0 on anything else.<P>I am house hunting at present, and everytime I think of how much it is going to cost, I cringe, but I will find a way to make it work.  <P>Your plan, however, probably wont work.  Unless your are going buy a house, live with your parents, and never leave your parents house....<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
