Z||000006||Formula||04-30-2001||10:45 PM||||ok, I guess my 2 mins on thinking about it didnt pay off lol....<P>what are the IRAs tat everyone keps talking about?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ferg||05-01-2001||09:14 AM||||there are 2 types - traditional and roth IRA's. These are just retirement accounts. There are significant differences between the two so you'd have to check which is the best for your situation. The best way to do this is to get information from your local bank, or places like - paine webber, oppenheimer funds, kemper mutual funds. The web sites of these fund companies will tell you the differences better than I can. -OR- If the company you work for has a 401k for you to take part in, you can talk to the fund manager and get specific questions answered.<P>Hope this helps. <p>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||05-01-2001||09:22 AM||||IRA's are the greatest.  The traditional IRA uses pretax dollars as the investment, so if you place $2000 into an IRA account, you get to deduct $2000 from your taxable income when you file.  The money will grow tax DEFERRED until you withdraw it at age 59 1/2, or you pay dearly in penalties. (the taxes you would have paid + a 10% penalty for early withdrawal) When you take the money out you will pay income tax at your current tax rate.<P>The Roth IRA you invest with after tax dollars, however this money will grow tax exempt when you withdraw it at 59 1/2.<P>Depending on your age, the Roth IRA is a better investment for the young.  The caveat with these investment is that you donot, DO NOT want to take any distributions out once they are put in or you will lose the investment gains.<P>These are both long term investment opportunities and can be invested in many forms (usually mutual funds).  I would recommend seeing an investment professional to set up a plan to invest in no-load mutual funds.  The earlier you start the better.<P>Once you start working, get into your companies 401K plan if available, another taxed deferred investment opportunity.<P>There are hardship distribution that can be taken but you will pay the penalties.<P>Of course our intellegent elected officials can change the rules at any time, but if you count on Social Security to take care of you in your "old age"  don't.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000009||AusFiero||05-01-2001||10:06 AM||||I don't see anything in the budget for fast cars, fast women and booze????<P>Lets face it...ya can still buy houses when ya old...The fast women are harder to find and the booze doesn't go down as well then either.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS. I had a ball when I was young. Partied travelled and did anything I wanted not worrying about the consequences. Didn't stop me being successful now that I am 35. Rethink a little...lifes to short to worry about money as the number 1. <BR>Oh and all the posts above are right...it don't work out like your plan.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JSocha||05-01-2001||10:47 AM||||If you do work and your company offers an IRA (either 401K or SIMPLE IRA) take it!<P>If your company offers a SIMPLE IRA (better IMO as long as the company can qualify for this plan) definatly take it as you are able to put away upto $6K per year which also reduces your tax liability to Uncle Sam per pay check.  The amount (percentage or fixed amount) is taken from your gross pay each paycheck leaving you with the net pay which is then taxable (ie, tax deferred).<P>Have to earn $5K in a prior year to qualify.  However, as long as you put in 3% or more of your "gross income" your company will typically match up to 3% and put it into the account.  However, if you put in only lets say 2 1/2%, then the company only has to match 2 1/2%.<P>Hmmmmm?  Lets see how that would equate:  $6K (or approx. $500.00/month) that I put in + 3% the company matches (providing I have put in 3% or more) and that they put into my IRA account (or $1,800) = $7,800.00 per year - INVESTED and as long as I have put in my minimum of 3% (set as a percentage deduction) or more of my gross wages or a monthly "fixed" deduction from my paycheck.<P>Now, with $7,800.00 invested over the year and being paid interest anywhere between 5% to 22% per year (varies depending on the market and investment - weak or agressive) return on this investment, you will be much farther ahead in the long run and potentially earn more then your calculated 800K for selling houses after your so called plan of 25 years.<P>Another option is setting up your own independent ROTH IRA which is tax sheltered.  Put in $2K a year into this account (preferably at the beginning of a new year so it can be invested wisely and make money over the year) and your investment agent will ensure that it is placed into tax sheltered anuities.<P>Now look at your plan (amounts) and what I have just stated here (amounts) and see with which you will be farther ahead with and without burdensome expenses.<P><B>DJRice:</B>  If you do find your house and are paying off the monthly payments, but yet want to pay it off sooner (without paying more and still meeting your monthly payment obligation), let me know and I will show you a very simple trick (if you don't know about it) that the lending institutions do not tell you about that will allow you to pay it off years sooner and save money that would otherwise be going towards the interest on the loan!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||sameul||05-01-2001||04:47 PM||||  These are matters I've looked into and thought about a great deal(though I'm no expert).  Roth IRA are a gift from god(actually a gift from the government which is rarer).  Put your money in a roth IRA when you're young, if you have young children put money into their roth IRAs(theres kind of a legal loophole with this your kids are supposed to earn the money, but if they earn less than i think its $6000 they don't have to report it to the government, so did they earn it? who knows, of course double check with competant legal and tax advisors).  If you start young enough (say 18) investing and assume HISTORICAL AVERAGES (not hoping for amazing returns) of the stock market you can be a millionaire(in todays dollars) by the time you retire.  I'm not going to go through the math of it right now but I will say I know my math well.  Before I went to college I received a perfect 800 on my SAT math section and a 5 on my calculus AB test.  To invest in a roth IRA probably the best thing to do is call Vanguard or go to their website at vanguard.com and get them to send you an application for a roth ira and info on their S and p 500 fund.  The paperwork is simple to fill out.  Put $2000 away religiously and ignore the flucuations in the stock market (times will be good and times will be bad).<BR>  Houses are much trickier.  Potential problems include:  destructive renters, low vacancy rates, high maintenance, decreasing house values and deteriorating neighboorhoods(its amazing sometimes even ritzy neighboorhoods with huge houses can become a ghetto).<BR>  I do think investing in real estate is much better than going into business because there are less skills you need to be succesful and it is harder to lose your shirt.  But if you do want to invest in properties think about borrowing the majority of the purchase price(putting a 30% downpayent is a good idea).  You can enhance your returns an amazing amount while getting a huge tax write off.  Ignore people who say that banks are making a mint off mortgages or that you should make an extra payment every month to pay off the loan early--they don't understand the time value of money.  Even though you may end up paying several times the houses purchase price you are paying most of it back far in the futureand in the meantime collecting rent from renters.  I would gladly pay anyone back $200,000 in fifty years if they loan me $100,000 today.<BR>  One last thing it is good that you are at least thinking of this now most people I know who get out of high school or college the first thing they do is buy a new car (should I say buy a loan) which drops in value like a rock and they learn to spend money!  Good luck!||||reg||14
Z||000012||Slammed Fiero||05-01-2001||05:51 PM||||I live in a city with a big university (Queens , it's like harvard of the north) in anycase I own(ed?) one rental house that I bought 3 years ago with some found money , like a lot of people are pointing out there are a lot of unforseen costs , new roofs , furnace and such (property tax , re-assessments). Well managed it can pay it self off rather quickly , and you can write off quite a few things against the house , but it is a chore sometimes.. I just put my house on the market , real estate values in the neighbourhood went up , I put a lot of my own hard work into the house , I have had one offer on the house in the last 2 weeks , and it's still pending due to circumstances , I stand to make a good chunk of change on the property even after the mortgage is paid off (open ended mortgage) was it worth it? yeah , could it have back fired ..Oh hell yeah . I got lucky I hit a good patch in our local economy and I decided to get out before I go to the next stage in my education<P>Planning is the Key in this situation , take everything into consideration. Real estate can be a nice stable investment , but it can also bite you in the ass.<P>Just research all the alternatives. If I were to do it again , I would have bought a franchise like Subway , more overhead , but managed well it can be a good little money maker. Subway in Canada has fairly low entry costs and don't have a "sealed Deal" like larger franchises such as Mcdonalds (1 million capital needed to start , 20 year contract , they will buy you the land and build the building, they base you salary as a part owner upon sales (thats Mcdonalds) <P>Just look at the trends in the neighbourhood you plan to buy in , check when the property was last assessed , my property is due for a re assessment in 6 months , I know the taxes will go up due to the area being built up and the state the house is in.<P>hope this helps , keep in mind I'm 23, and somewhat new to this. <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||05-01-2001||06:18 PM||||I'm really not trying to start a flame war so don't take it as such...<P>Sameul and Slammed Fiero, "I don't know how you dream, but when I dream, I don't dream in <B>Technicolor</B>."  That is what I feel FORMULA is dreaming in with his written plan.<P>Slammed Fiero, I admit you did get lucky especially if you can get more for your property due to possible development in that area.  I wonder where FORMULA is considering on purchasing this so called property he has written in his plan?<P>I also admit, at least he is writing out a plan which most successful people do so they know where they are going, what they want to achieve (goals) and a plan on how to get there and to check if they are on check.  Just needs to be more realistic.<P>I've been a Landlord twice of properties I owned and I got out of it because it was a nightmare...expense wise and paper wise!  <P>I sold the properties just to get rid of them.  Thankfully at a marginal profit over the original purchase price.<P>Have another property I still own on 10 acres, after the wife and I recently upgraded and bought a larger house on quite a few acres of property (8.5 acres) next to a golf course including property and barn for our horses (31 acres).  <P>People are trying to rent from me my old property.  No way...no how.  Been there done that.  Toilet plugs up (mind you that it doesn't overflow) at 3AM in the morning, suddenly, as the landlord, they graciously call you to come out with "your" $5.00 plunger to unplug it so they can get some sleep...hense possibly the reason I don't dream in Technicolor as I am always awaken before the good shows start playing on the inside of my eyelids after the Three Stooges pre-cinema events play.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||1
